 The session cookie is also applied to error responses, so a request that throws still slides the window and still persists a session created during it.
 
+## Rotate the Password
+
+To change `password` without signing everyone out, keep the previous password in `extraUnsealPasswords`. Extra passwords only open sessions; new seals always use `password`.
+
+```js
+const session = await useSession(event, {
+  password: process.env.SESSION_PASSWORD_NEW,
+  extraUnsealPasswords: [process.env.SESSION_PASSWORD_OLD],
+});
+```
+
+1. Put the new password in `password` and move the old one to `extraUnsealPasswords`.
+2. A cookie session opened with an extra password is resealed with `password` in the same response, so active sessions migrate on their next request.
+3. Once sessions have migrated, remove the old password. `maxAge` or `idleTimeout` gives a natural deadline: after that long, sessions that never came back are expired anyway.
+
+Passwords are tried in order, `password` first. Extra passwords need the same entropy as `password`.
+
+> [!IMPORTANT]
+> Keep the list short: every extra password adds a PBKDF2 attempt for each invalid cookie. If a password is compromised, remove it from the list right away, since sessions sealed with it stay valid while it is listed.
+
+> [!NOTE]
+> Sessions read from the session header are opened with extra passwords too, but are not resealed.
+
 ## Use Multiple Sessions
 
 Because each session is stored under its own `name`, you can run several independent sessions on the same request. They live in separate cookies and never overwrite each other, which is useful for keeping unrelated concerns apart, such as a long-lived auth session and a short-lived flash message:
